<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Niklotings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Mecklenburg, also called the Niklotings, was a dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>, a prince of the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> who died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in 1160. His son Pribislav accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, and the family ruled Mecklenburg as lords, as dukes from 1348 and as grand dukes from 1815. Its lines in Mecklenburg-Schwerin and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> reigned until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="House of Mecklenburg" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glasshouse, using elements by Sodacan, Trondivers · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Mecklenburg_-_Schwerin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Niklotings</b>, descendants of the Obotrite prince Niklot, had ruled the Lordship of Mecklenburg since the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After the extinction of the Güstrow line in 1695, a succession dispute in the <b>House of Mecklenburg</b> was settled by the Treaty of Hamburg in 1701.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a></li></ul></section>
